{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Patchset 2 pushed — CI failure fixed (it was mine) + three review findings addressed\n\nChange 2647, patchset **2**, sha `1f4fd079d5ae`, `Change-Id` preserved\n(`I60660c0df17e00f9e3f796e98a97f51a02e2249b`). Pushing outdated the PS1 votes, as expected;\nawaiting fresh `LLM-Review` and `Verified`. Not submitted.\n\n### The CI failure — mine, not a flake\n\n`build-and-test / pytest (ubuntu-latest, py3.13)`, run 33936088543:\n\n```\n1 failed, 19482 passed, 78 skipped in 1296.04s\nFAILED tests/unit/test_tests_import_convention.py::test_no_tests_rooted_imports_anywhere_under_tests\n  tests/unit/test_opcert_binding_thread_propagation_ff4a.py:28:\n      from tests.unit._opcert_helpers import ...\n```\n\n**Exactly one failure, and it names my new file.** My test used a `tests.`-rooted import, which\nthis repo forbids: *\"`tests.`-rooted imports resolve only when the repository root is on\n`sys.path`, which the bare `pytest` console script (the invocation used by `make test` and CI)\ndoes not provide.\"*\n\nIt passed locally because I ran `python -m pytest`, which puts cwd on `sys.path`; the bare\n`pytest` console script CI uses does not. Reproduced locally with the bare script, fixed to the\nrepo convention (`from _opcert_helpers import keypair` — the same form used by\n`test_attest_opcert.py:21` and `test_attest_opcert_heldout.py:26`), and re-run bare: green.\n\n**Not pre-existing, not a flake** — a deterministic, self-inflicted convention violation. Nothing\nto file separately. My own process error: I verified with `python -m pytest` and never with the\ninvocation the contract is written against.\n\n### (a) The guard silently no-opped on a broken trust root — you were right, fixed\n\nThis was the same defect class the change exists to fix, so I did not defer it.\n\n`load_trusted_environments` distinguishes the two cases **deliberately**: an **absent** config\nreturns `None` (fail-open, legitimate); a **present-but-malformed/unreadable** one raises\n`TrustedEnvError`. My blanket `except Exception → set()` collapsed both into \"nothing pinned\",\nso a corrupted trust root disabled the guard with nothing anywhere saying so.\n\n`_pinned_public_key_bodies` now returns `set[str] | None` and distinguishes **three** states:\n\n| state | return | caller |\n|---|---|---|\n| principal not pinned (no keyring) | `None` | no-op — every dev box, and the absent-config fail-open |\n| pinned, with active keys | that set | refuse unless the signing key is in it |\n| **trust root unreadable/malformed** | **raises `OpcertKeyUnavailable`** | refuse — pinned-ness is *unknown* |\n\nBlast radius checked: an **absent** config still returns `None` and never raises, so a checkout\nwith no pin file is unaffected. Only a present-but-broken file refuses — and that file is\nGerrit-gated and CODEOWNERS-protected, so it cannot drift casually, and a loud refusal is the\ncorrect response to a corrupted security artifact.\n\n### (b) Revoked keys — verified against the contract, then fixed\n\nConfirmed real, and I checked it before changing behaviour as you asked. `trusted_env_keyring`\nreturns every record verbatim including `revoked_at_log_position`; the **verify** path handles\nrevocation separately via era anchoring (`verify_required_environment` →\n`key_not_valid_at_era`), which my sign-time guard has no storage anchor to do.\n\nThe reasoning that makes exclusion safe rather than merely stricter: a revoked key is revoked as\nof a **past** log position, and a signature minted **now** is necessarily later, so it can never\nproduce a cert that verifies at its own storage anchor. Filtering revoked keys out is therefore a\nstrict tightening that cannot reject anything that would otherwise have worked.\n\nNote the interaction with (a): if a principal's keys are **all** revoked, the set is empty —\nwhich is **not** \"unpinned\". That principal is pinned and has no key capable of producing a\nverifiable cert, so it refuses. That is why the helper returns `None` for unpinned rather than\noverloading the empty set.\n\n**Zero production impact:** both live entries in `.rebar/trusted_environments.yaml`\n(`9f1c8e42-…` and `ade0f2ef-…`) carry `revoked_at_log_position: null`.\n\n### (c) The structural guard now resolves import aliases\n\nNew `_thread_spawn_names(tree)` walks the module's `Import` / `ImportFrom` nodes and resolves\nevery local name bound to `threading.Thread` — `import threading`, `import threading as th`,\n`from threading import Thread`, `from threading import Thread as T` — then matches calls against\nthat resolved set instead of the literal name `Thread`. A companion test pins the resolver\nitself, including a negative case (`from concurrent.futures import Thread` → not matched).\n\n### Nothing declined\n\nAll three were actioned. The fourth advisory finding was not raised to me and I did not act on it.\n\n### Verification\n\nMutation checks, all under `sandbox-exec` denying writes outside the worktree — **four** seeded\ndefects, each returning **exactly one** test to RED, restored green after each:\n\n| seeded defect | RED test |\n|---|---|\n| drop the `ctx.run` context copy | `test_async_gate_daemon_signs_under_the_bound_key` |\n| remove the revocation filter (b) | `test_mint_refuses_when_the_only_pinned_key_is_revoked` |\n| swallow the unreadable-trust-root error (a) | `test_mint_refuses_when_the_pin_config_is_unreadable` |\n| neuter the divergence guard | `test_mint_refuses_a_cert_whose_key_is_not_pinned_…` |\n\n(b)'s first RED run used a `_pin` helper that silently wrote `null` instead of the revoked\nposition, so that test was red for the wrong reason; I corrected the helper and re-established\nRED via the mutation above before accepting it.\n\n`make lint` passed — complexity `new=0 increased=0`, mechanism `new=0`. `make typecheck` clean\n(762 files). Targeted regression, run with the **bare `pytest`** CI uses: **96 passed, 2 skipped**\nacross the op-cert signing/verify/trusted-env/startup-binding family, the MCP gate suites, the\nbugfix-size gate, and the import-convention contract. `make verify` not run (host load); CI is\nthe authority.\n\nNo orphaned processes (`check_orphaned_load.py` clean)."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788575231244328556,"uuid":"12aefbfb-3109-432e-983b-c104a59b3c54"}